    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d757-a1">

                <pb facs="0783" n="525" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg525"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure—Telegram—Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the German Minister
                        of Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Von Jagow</hi>)</head>
                <p>168. With regard to the confidential negotiations which I hope soon to be able to
                    take up with the American Government, it seems that the last paragraph of the
                    American <hi rendition="#italic">Frye</hi> note of 10th inst,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d757-f1"/> contains a suggestion which may prove useful in the
                    general negotiations concerning <hi rendition="#italic">Lusitania</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Arabic</hi>. I understand the suggestion so that we
                    should refrain from attacking passenger ships without warning pending
                    negotiations, which would put the burden on England to refrain from unlawful
                    blockade pending negotiations. It does, however, not mean that we should give up
                    the whole submarine warfare.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
